characters are concerned so you'll see  that the cursor goes into this emoji now  if you know I want to live adventurous  in a sense yeah so you could add  something here and it breaks up the  surrogate pair but don't do that yeah so  that's Unicode support we also worked a  bit on tokenization of code or text in  general and the spacing around tokens so  this is a screenshot from a previous  cloud versions from previous code  versions and this is what you would see  now so notice that the spacing got a  little more relaxed and it's definitely  more similar to the desktop now we also  change  font that we're using under the hood for  friending some of these special  characters there is still some small  differences to the desktop but that  might actually be bridged by changing  the desktop not not the cloud at some  point desktop might be using the same  font we're using now to render these  characters so yeah lots of things going  on I can't go into detail for all of  them but the the overall focus of us has  certainly been no improved ability  robustness and performance in the cloud  and two projects I want to want to  highlight in particular because I think  they're quite interesting is first of  all client-side graphics rendering so so  far what we did to render any sort of  graphics in cloud notebooks was to use  the servers at kernel and front-end to  generate some JSON data that we would  then turn into an SVG graphic in the  browser and that was a good approach  because it allowed us to support all  sorts of graphics right away but it did  have that performance overhead of going  to the kernel every time we render the  graphic so what we've started to build  is a complete implementation of the  whole graphics layer in the JavaScript  front-end on the one hand for better  performance obviously but also on the  other hand to support for instance  dynamic in places where it didn't work  before so in this case we have you know  a circle with a dynamic radius like here  and then two other circles that are just  sitting there statically and they also  have a slider that changes that radius  so now you can see that updating  dynamically and that's something that  didn't actually work before you would  have to wrap dynamic around the graph  the whole graphic expression which then  had also negative consequences for  performance so this is something that's  that's starting to work well now and in  this case for this simple dynamic it  doesn't even have to go back to the  kernel which is which is very  the only caveat is that so far we only  support discs and circles so it's it's a  very small initial step on the surface  but under the hood we really build out a  nice framework and we should be able to  to add more primitives quite soon will  probably take a little longer to get to  things like inserts and axes but the  good thing is that you know there's  always a fallback solution so all  graphics will certainly render in the  cloud if necessary by by using that  kernel based approach we have now right  the other performance project I want to  talk about briefly is the compiler in  the cloud and this is not working quite  yet but in the next version of the cloud  we will have a working compiler so you  can do things like this now I can't use  the Wolfram cloud comm because it's not  quite there yet but on my local cloud  instance I could create a new notebook  and evaluate this and and this will do  the right thing so I can this and and it  just works in the cloud as well so  that's certainly good to speed up  Wolfram language code in in cloud  kernels but it will also allow us to  build something which is you know I  already showed this last year it's not  what it's not done yet but this is this  really the first step to getting this to  work where the idea is that we can  compile certain manipulates so need to  enable this and then take this  manipulate and I think I usually have to  do it twice for some reason but now what  should be happening maybe it's maybe I  was asking for too much  yeah I think it's it's quite smooth so  so what's happening now is that it took  this expression inside the plot compiled  it to webassembly  which can run purely in the browser  without going back to the kernel and it  uses that to update the plot of a never  attract the slider so this this allows  for very smooth interactions that don't  suffer from the usual latency between  the client and the server side cloud  infrastructure so that's again a  long-term thing we're working on  enabling the compiler in the cloud is  the first step to that I'm running out  of time so I can't go into too much  detail but we've also worked on this  wolfram notebook and by the library you  can go to the website check out the  documentation it basically allows you to  seamlessly embed notebooks on websites  and in terms of integration we've also  worked on integration into Google Drive  so you can go to go to Google Drive if  you have a notebook file there just open  it and either you already have the  Wolfram cloud app connected or once you  do that it will suggest to open it and  we can do that really quickly I can just  say open this with Wolfram cloud it will  copy the file to the cloud open it there  you can already can edit edit the  notebook here if you want and then save  it back to Google Drive that's also a  nice integration particularly for  Chromebooks for instance so yeah that's  all I wanted to show today please reach  out either by email or in community  where we try to be quite active and also  if you want to learn more about each  release of the cloud we started to  publish these cloud release notes which  have lots of detail about each of our  releases looking forward to what you all  creating with the cloud and thanks for  your attention  you  [Applause]
 

